From: Ruizhi Zhong <ruizhi.zhong88@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 20:45:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] kvmem: stop NVML's DEPRECATED macro clobbering llama.cpp's

monitor_hardware.hpp includes <nvml.h> whenever it is available. NVML
declares DEPRECATED(ver) as a one-argument macro, while llama.cpp's llama.h
declares DEPRECATED(func, hint) with two arguments and both are plain macros,
so whichever lands second breaks the other. A translation unit that takes the
KVMem monitor header and later the llama.cpp API -- tools/kvmem-vision.h
includes mtmd.h, which wraps three declarations in DEPRECATED(func, hint) --
fails to compile:

    mtmd.h:270:61: error: macro "DEPRECATED" passed 2 arguments, but takes just 1

llama.h is included first in these translation units, so its definition is
already gone by the time NVML overwrites the name and cannot come back: the
include guard keeps it from being redefined. The definition is therefore
restored here, spelled the same way llama.h spells it for the same compilers,
and NVML's own uses are all expanded before this point.

Upstream-Status: Not submitted (reported while packaging 0.19.1)
--- a/kvmem/include/kvmem/monitor_hardware.hpp
+++ b/kvmem/include/kvmem/monitor_hardware.hpp
@@ -13,6 +13,20 @@
 #endif
 #if __has_include(<nvml.h>)
 #include <nvml.h>
+// NVML declares DEPRECATED(ver) with one argument and llama.cpp's llama.h
+// declares DEPRECATED(func, hint) with two. Both are plain macros, so the
+// header that comes second breaks the other one, and llama.h cannot repair
+// itself afterwards because its include guard is already set. Restore the
+// llama.cpp spelling for the compilers llama.h has a branch for; NVML has
+// finished with the name at this point.
+#undef DEPRECATED
+#ifdef __GNUC__
+#    define DEPRECATED(func, hint) func __attribute__((deprecated(hint)))
+#elif defined(_MSC_VER)
+#    define DEPRECATED(func, hint) __declspec(deprecated(hint)) func
+#else
+#    define DEPRECATED(func, hint) func
+#endif
 #define KVMEM_MONITOR_NVML 1
 #endif
 
